b/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194710 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194710 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186194710 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "600f7255-9cdc-55b5-b56b-0668aa15e5f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198076 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12562" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198076 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the quadratic Taylor polynomial for the function of two variables \n$f(x,y) = \\cos(x+y)$\nabout the point $(0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198076 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c5e3d93-e244-52da-845b-365bb94bd19d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198076 #edb/ref 1074 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y) = (0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}((-1)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-1)\\cdot xy+(-1)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-xy-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198077 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12563" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198077 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf\\left(x,y \\right) \n=\nx^2y-xy^3,\n$$\n\ndetermine the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](images/0d/0de380d24ed675cff86b219a2a4a337699d68e168fc875475158523022915b0c.svg) Give your answer in matrix form." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198077 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d13a6c0-0912-5089-81ed-c298bf747ccd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198077 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n![](images/61/61c5a4204c4ad078bd036eee7770e28faf4741bedb274a54d08d54dae43fbc68.svg) \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n![](images/ab/ab531b6a5468a0ddb8cf46a42300f19cc6406c94c011ae814d60af24f66e2aa0.svg)\n\nand\n\n![](images/3d/3dbb0d517f6875d4a0601ba284878cb60ac0b615491564ae9458c34c0f3de403.svg)\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2xy-{y}^{3} & {x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x-3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial (in matrix form) is given by\n\n![](images/3c/3cbbec95c6327e77a53bdeea5de4a93481ca9f4164ebae0cb99bf28468513a12.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186198078 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12564" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198078 #edb/ref 1071 "For the twice-differentiable function $f:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,0) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,0,0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(1,0,0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](images/31/31305b4a4a27141bae70eabbb04acaf98928c275f8efec37ef8a869c7599a2f3.svg) to approximate the value of $f(1,1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186198078 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "137d7caf-b232-5e21-9583-14d907a98624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198078 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n![](images/a7/a7a5f8961c77ad8a606ce57a543d5606b834061f3a0777b074a5711dcc9a4529.svg) \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n![](images/27/276fd591c58ea0bd80eb0d3c236ade53fcd1d38864ccd650873cf052561b7586.svg)\n\nand\n\n![](images/06/06c98e6248f7fdb303c04772921b0423f8e2f88450d330c295bc3a72846ef858.svg)\n\nIn this case, we have ![](images/3a/3ac30d1603a502de1404a1888f7c410d22ae93b8b47db6694734a09634a43954.svg) and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n![](images/af/afd14fa251e88edb40ea71b70c56db5a03ec83d906608930e2b3ba3ba9c9fb0b.svg)\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at ![](images/1a/1af733b173aa4a358cd8c0b5c2754431be57887e7c43b0aa98125deefdecc4ad.svg) we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1) & ≈-1+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-1+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{2}{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-1+1+\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432374 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198434 #edb/ref 1039 "Findin