] [17592186281096 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281096 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186280201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281096 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9241f691-955a-5b21-a8a2-fb377b58fa65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281146 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186278873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281146 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281146 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281146 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281146 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281146 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281146 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281146 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186278873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281146 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b152db49-e069-50cf-bae6-a47ce8602ca1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281220 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186278673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281220 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281220 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281220 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281220 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281220 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281220 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281220 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186279071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281220 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dd3902cd-4ea0-53f7-8e46-8bbcf944e194" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281594 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281594 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163088" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281594 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281594 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)={e}^{xy},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=a+bxy.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281594 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "40019177-315b-5f8c-afeb-29b9f1c22ad3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281594 #edb/ref 1074 "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =y{e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{xy} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =0 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =(1+xy){e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}{e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(0\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(1)\\cdot xy+0\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+1xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=1+1=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
